<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nabeshima clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nabeshima were a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> family of Hizen province in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyushu">Kyushu</a> who ruled the Saga domain throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a>. Originally chief retainers of the Ryuzoji, they took over their lords' lands under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naoshige">Nabeshima Naoshige</a> after 1584. The family sponsored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arita">Arita</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> and, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabeshima-naomasa">Nabeshima Naomasa</a> in the 1850s, Western industrial technology, and Saga men played a leading part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nabeshima-clan.jpg" alt="Nabeshima clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_Nabesima_Gyouyou.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
